+# Agent Note: Web-terminal capability seam for the browser Terminal view
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-16-web-terminal-capability-seam.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The web GUI has no way for a human to open an interactive shell. The persistent-terminal capability (`ctx.terminals` + `terminal-bash` + `tool-terminal`) exists only for the model: it is a send/wait registry that waits for a prompt marker after each `startSend`, and it is composed per-preset behind an `isolate` realm. A human typing freely needs the opposite shape — raw streaming output, fire-and-forget input — and the host gateway (apiproxy) must reach the sessions, which a per-preset realm hides. The planned Terminal tab (beside Chat and Trajectory) needs a host-plane, per-session terminal the browser can open, read, and drive, without disturbing the model-facing surface.
+
+## Decision
+
+**A new host-plane seam, `ctx.webTerminals`, with a `-local` backend, a polling `terminal` wire domain, and an xterm.js client tab.**
+
+- `packages/host/web-terminal` is the Service Definition: the abstract `WebTerminalService` with `open(sessionId, cwd?)` (idempotent — reuses the live terminal) and `get(sessionId)`. `WebTerminalSession` exposes `read(cursor)` (bounded scrollback delta with an opaque cursor, `truncated`, and `exited`), `write(data)`, `signal(signal)`, and `close()`. The seam is polling-oriented rather than send/wait because the human types freely.
+- `packages/host/web-terminal-local` implements it over `ctx.subprocess.spawnTerminal` (node-pty, ConPTY on Windows). The shell is selected by host platform: PowerShell on Windows (resolved through `resolvePwshPath`), bash elsewhere. Output streams raw — ANSI escapes intact — into a bounded scrollback; the emulator, not the backend, renders escapes.
+- The wire: a `terminal` domain in apiproxy (`terminal.open` / `terminal.write` / `terminal.read` / `terminal.signal` / `terminal.close`) with closed error codes `terminal-unavailable` / `terminal-not-found` / `terminal-spawn-failed` / `terminal-exited`, all carrying `{ sessionId }`. apiproxy reaches the seam through `ctx.get('webTerminals')` (optional — the gateway boots without it, and the domain answers `terminal-unavailable`), and closes each terminal on `session/disposed`.
+- The browser tab (`packages/client/ui-terminal`) fills the `conversation.view` ring as id `terminal` (order 20, beside chat and trajectory). It renders xterm.js, opens/reattaches on mount, seeds the emulator from the retained scrollback, polls `terminal.read` every 60ms, and forwards keystrokes through `terminal.write`. Unmounting disposes only the emulator — the host terminal stays alive, so a tab switch re-attaches to the same shell. A restart button closes the host terminal and reopens it fresh.
+- The terminal is a human-facing surface: its I/O never reaches a model request, so no session event or model notice is required (model-visible ⟺ logged is vacuous here).
+- `tsdown.client.ts` gains plain-`.css` side-effect inlining (resolved through the module graph) so `@xterm/xterm/css/xterm.css` bundles into the single-file client artifact; `.module.css` hashing is unchanged.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Reuse the model-facing `terminals` seam** — one registry; rejected because its `TerminalBackendSession` is send/wait (prompt-detect, exclusive active send), and the service is per-preset behind an isolate realm that the host gateway cannot reach. Extending it would pollute the model contract with streaming semantics.
+- **Stream output over the mux** — crisp delivery; deferred to a follow-up. Polling at 60ms keeps the v1 surface minimal (no `MuxFrame` variants, no runtime mux dispatch), and `terminal.write` echo returns fast enough for typing.
+- **Spawn node-pty directly in the provider** — full control of TERM and resize; rejected because it would bypass the subprocess seam's process-tree cleanup and sandbox integration, re-implementing what `terminal-bash` already does carefully.
+- **A Typert Remote** — remotes target agent-adjacent capabilities; the Terminal view is GUI host surface like `files.*`/`workspace.*`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- **Bought**: a human can open, read, and drive a real interactive shell per session, with the same subprocess/PTY substrate the agent uses; the terminal survives view switches and session switching; the wire surface is closed, typed, and bounded, and a gateway without the provider degrades to a single `terminal-unavailable` answer instead of failing load.
+- **Cost**: a new seam plus a wire domain and a new browser dependency (`@xterm/xterm`); the v1 is polling rather than streaming, has a fixed terminal size, inherits the subprocess seam's `TERM=dumb` (no color/full-screen programs), and supports one terminal per session — all documented Known Limitations deferred to a follow-up.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `web-terminal-local` service/resolve specs drive the backend over a scripted `SubprocessTerminalHandle` (open/reuse, cursor reads with truncation and exit, write/signal forwarding, close, platform shell selection).
+- `api-proxy-terminal.spec.ts` drives `createApiProxy` over a scripted seam: wire mapping and rpcId echo, reattach, `terminal-unavailable` / `terminal-not-found` / `terminal-spawn-failed` / `terminal-exited` / `cancelled`, and the write/read/signal/close round-trip.
+- `ui-terminal` apply spec pins the view-ring registration and injected callbacks; the component spec drives a mocked xterm (mount/seed, keystroke forwarding, poll delta write, unavailable/exited banners, restart close+reopen, unmount-dispose-without-close).
+- Gates: repository typecheck, `verify-cordis-config`, `verify-package-invariants`, `verify-cordis-catalog` (SERVICE_PAGE + type-link exemptions), `verify-doc-graphs` (seam classification), `verify-package-readme-model-experience` (three new `none` entries), and the client-bundle purity gate (xterm inlines, `@deepseek-ai` cross-plugin value imports stay forbidden).
