+# Agent Note: Workspace-files capability seam for the browser file panel
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-16-workspace-files-capability-seam.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The web GUI offers no way for a human to browse or read the current workspace's files. The agent's file tools (`tool-fs`, `tool-fs-search`) are model-facing and scoped to tool executions, and the [directory-picker seam](2026-07-28-directory-picker-capability-seam.md) lists directories only for Workspace adoption. The planned right-side file panel (tree view, then editor) needs a host-side read surface that observes the same filesystem backend, typed errors, and freshness versions as the agent's own tools, stays bounded for wire safety, and slots into the closed apiproxy contract without touching agent-facing surfaces.
+
+## Decision
+
+**A new seam, `ctx.workspaceFiles`, with one `-fs` backend, exposed as a new `files` wire domain.**
+
+- `packages/host/workspace-files` is the Service Definition: the abstract `WorkspaceFiles` service with `list(path, signal)` and `read(path, signal)`. The seam has one interaction shape, so it exposes methods rather than a capability union (unlike directory-picker, whose backends differ in interaction, not just mechanism).
+- `packages/host/workspace-files-fs` implements it over `ctx.fs` — deliberately not Node's stdlib: the panel reuses `resolve`/`stat`/`listDir`/`readText`, the `FsError` vocabulary, and the freshness versions the tool layer sees. `FileContent.version` is the token the later guarded whole-file write (the editor's save path) will use for stale protection.
+- The wire: a `files` domain in apiproxy (`files.list`, `files.read`) with closed error codes `files-not-found` / `files-not-directory` / `files-not-file` / `files-too-large` / `files-permission-denied` / `files-io-error`, all carrying `{ path }`; the read bound rides the message. `WorkspaceFilesError` maps 1:1 onto these codes (the directory-picker precedent).
+- The browser panel (`packages/client/ui-files`) fills a new `conversation.details.files` seat that ui-conversation's details entry declares beside the tool seat — a two-tab panel (Tools | Files) with no layout change. The panel resolves the session's workspace root from the standard workspace feed, loads tree levels lazily over `files.list` (directories first, truncation flagged), and edits one text file in a CodeMirror editor: reads over `files.read`, Save (button or Ctrl/Cmd+S) commits over `files.write` with the version the panel read, and a `files-stale-version` failure surfaces a conflict banner with a reload affordance. It drives `ctx.connection.api` directly (no runtime service wraps the files.* domain) and registers no store: expanded/selected/editor state is component-private viewing state, reset with the panel's per-session remount, because the details panel closes on session switch. The details panel opens through a Details pill on a tool row's expanded body — the same owner chain as the trajectory Inspect pill (ChatView → chat node seat → ToolCallTree → ToolRow), so the wiring adds `openDetailsCall` end to end rather than relying on the previously dead `openDetails` inject face.
+- Human edits reach the model through the established injected-context path: the gateway logs a `user/file-edit` session event (durable audit record) and queues an `agent.inject()` system-reminder notice, which the loop claims and logs as an ordinary `user/message` at the next pre-step — model-visible ⟺ logged holds without a new surface-event kind.
+- The editor is **CodeMirror, not Monaco**: the vscode-style Monaco editor needs a worker pipeline (editor/language workers) that the single-file client-bundle architecture (one `lib/client.js` factory per plugin, no code splitting) does not carry; CodeMirror bundles workerless. The panel binds three keymaps through a toolbar selector — CodeMirror default, vim (`@replit/codemirror-vim`), and emacs (`@replit/codemirror-emacs`) — and a keymap switch remounts the editor while keeping the current buffer. The choice is panel-private viewing state, reset per session.
+- Bounds are config fields, not per-request: `maxEntries` (default 1000, the GitHub web-UI listing bound) and `maxReadBytes` (default 1 MiB); a cut listing reports `truncated`, an oversized read fails closed.
+- Hidden rows are flagged dot-prefix and returned; the client owns display (directory-picker stance). Whole-filesystem scope stays a Known Limitation, not a fence.
+- Composition: the web-app bundle mounts the `-fs` row before the API gateway, and apiproxy injects `workspaceFiles` as a required service — the browser surface always composes it.
+- Phase one is read-only on purpose: a human edit must also produce a session event (model-visible ⟺ logged) before the model can know about it, so `files.write` lands together with the `user/file-edit` event and its `deriveMessages` presentation in the editor phase.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Read Node's stdlib directly in the provider (directory-picker-browse style)** — one less dependency and no fs-service edge; rejected because it would fork file access: the panel would not share the fs backend's error vocabulary, binary rejection, or freshness versions, and the guarded save would need its own stale detection instead of reusing `FsVersion`.
+- **Extend the directory-picker seam with file rows** — fewer packages; rejected because the picker contract is interaction-shaped (`native` vs `browse`) and exists for Workspace adoption, while file access is a different consumer with different bounds and a write path.
+- **Per-request `maxEntries`/`maxReadBytes` fields** — caller-tunable bounds; rejected: the panel is the only consumer, and deployment-varying choices belong in validated Config fields (repo convention), not wire fields.
+- **One `files-error` code with a reason string** — fewer branches; rejected because the client needs to branch on not-found vs too-large vs permission vs stale for distinct UI, and closed typed codes are the apiproxy convention.
+- **A Typert Remote instead of an apiproxy domain** — the panel is GUI host surface like `host.*`/`workspace.*`; remotes target agent-adjacent capabilities and would drag the generator machinery into host-only reads.
+- **Direct `user/message` surface append for the edit notice** — one event instead of two; rejected because direct appends bypass the inbox the agent loop owns and its FIFO-conservation invariants, while `agent.inject()` is the sanctioned context path (agent-instructions precedent).
+- **Monaco as the editor** — the vscode-feel option the plan started with; rejected because its worker pipeline does not fit the single-file client-bundle architecture, and CodeMirror (workerless, bundler-friendly) also carries the planned vim/emacs keymaps.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- **Bought**: the panel observes the agent's own file channel — same backend, error vocabulary, and freshness versions — so the later guarded save path reuses `FsVersion` stale protection instead of building its own; the wire surface is closed, typed, and bounded by config, not request fields; a misconfigured browser composition fails loud at load because apiproxy requires the service.
+- **Cost**: a new seam plus a wire domain to maintain, where extending the picker would have added no packages; apiproxy's required-injection set grows by one; the seam is read-only, so a human edit's model visibility (`user/file-edit` event) is deliberately deferred to the editor phase; whole-filesystem scope stays an acknowledged limitation rather than a fence.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `workspace-files-fs` tests drive the backend over a real temporary tree (bounds and truncation, hidden flags, every error code, version round-trip, guarded create/update/stale writes), plus a scripted-stub suite for the error-mapping and boundary branches.
+- `api-proxy-files.spec.ts` drives `createApiProxy` over the real backend: wire mapping, rpcId echo, abort → `cancelled`, and the full write flow — guarded commit, the durable `user/file-edit` audit event, the inbox-queued model notice, stale rejection, and unknown-session refusal.
+- `seam.spec.ts` pins registration identity and the typed error.
+- `ui-files` apply spec pins the slot registration and the injected files.* callbacks; the component spec drives the panel with stubbed feeds (root resolution, lazy expansion with cached re-expand, selection preview, list/read/save error rendering, truncation flag, refresh, Ctrl/Meta+S, in-flight-save guard, keymap switch with buffer retention) and the editor spec drives a mocked CodeMirror seam (mount/destroy/doc-change relay, default/vim/emacs keymap composition).
+- Gates: repository typecheck, `verify-cordis-config`, `verify-package-invariants`, `verify-client-catalog` (slot catalog), `verify-persistence-catalog` (the new `user/file-edit` event), `verify-doc-graphs` (the seam's role classification), `verify-translation-pairing` for the README pairs, per-file 100% coverage for the new source trees.
